page cmd_cp {
  title = "vmlab cp"
  chapter_header "vmlab cp" {
    kicker       = "Commands · reference"
    reading_time = "4 min read"
    updated      = "2026-09-02"
    version      = "vmlab 0.9"
  }

  p <<DOC
    `vmlab cp` copies a file or a tree between the host and a guest over the
    vmlab agent's file session, with no guest network and no share involved.
    Either side may be a guest reference spelled `<vm>:<path>`; the other is
    a host path. Parent directories on the receiving side are created.
  DOC

  code sh { source = "vmlab cp <SRC> <DEST>" }

  table {
    rows:
      | "Option" | "Meaning" |
      | "`<SRC>`" | "Source: a host path, or `<vm>:<path>` to pull from the guest." |
      | "`<DEST>`" | "Destination: `<vm>:<path>` to push, or a host path when pulling." |
      | "`-h`, `--help`" | "Print help." |
  }

  p <<DOC
    The guest side is recognised by splitting on the first colon, so a
    Windows path keeps its drive letter: `box:C:/weave` is the machine `box`
    and the guest path `C:/weave`. The `<vm>` part accepts the `[lab/]name`
    form. When neither side is a guest reference the command is refused
    locally. A push whose host source does not exist is refused before any
    request is sent.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    A push sends one file, or walks a directory and sends every file under
    it to the matching path below the guest destination, one request per
    file, keeping each file's mode. The daemon opens the host file itself,
    so the path is made absolute first, and verifies the digest end to end.
    It prints `pushed <bytes> bytes to <vm>:<path>` for a file and
    `pushed <n> file(s), <bytes> bytes to <vm>:<path>` for a tree.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    A pull copies one guest file to the host path. When the host destination
    is an existing directory the guest file's name is kept under it. It
    prints `pulled <bytes> bytes to <path>`.
  DOC

  callout "Transfers run as the agent identity" {
    kind = :note
    body = "`cp` still runs as SYSTEM or root even on a machine that declares a `login {}`, unlike `exec` and `shell`. A pushed file is owned by the agent identity, not by the login you would attach as. To write into a login's home as that login, use `scp` over the SSH alias, or the `as_login` handle in a provision script (see [Logins and the SSH facade](guide_logins))."
  }

  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
vmlab cp ./tools/ dc01:C:/tools
vmlab cp dc01:C:/Windows/debug/netsetup.log ./logs/
vmlab cp mixed-lab/nix01:/etc/nixos/configuration.nix ./configuration.nix
SRC
  }

  p <<DOC
    Exit status is 0 on success. `not_found` (4) means the lab declares no
    machine by that name. `failed` (1) covers a machine that is not running,
    an agent that does not answer, a guest path that cannot be opened, and
    a digest mismatch. A malformed pair of arguments or a missing host
    source exits 1 before any request is sent.
  DOC
}
